<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Riobamba</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Riobamba is a city in the central highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimborazo">Chimborazo</a> Province. Founded by the Spanish in 1534 in the land of the Puruhá, it was the first Spanish city in what is now Ecuador. After an earthquake destroyed it in 1797 it was rebuilt on a new site, and in 1830 it hosted the constituent assembly that drew up the first constitution of independent Ecuador.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/riobamba.jpg" alt="Riobamba" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David C. S. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collage_Riobamba.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The country around Riobamba was inhabited by the Puruha before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> advanced into it in the late 15th century. The Puruha fiercely resisted the Inca conquest of what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, and the Inca Huayna Capac had to make an alliance to pacify the tribes that followed Condorazo, the Puruha general. He allied with the Schyris confederation, also called the Caran-Quitu people, who are believed to have developed the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>. The 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-velasco">Juan de Velasco</a> described the confederation as ruled by the Duchicela dynasty. Huayna Capac married the princess Paccha, and their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> became the last king of the Inca.</p><p>After the Spanish invasion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a> founded Riobamba on 15 August 1534 on the plains of San Miguel. It is considered the first city founded in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, and in 1563 it became part of the newly formed Royal Audience of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>. An earthquake destroyed it completely in 1797, and it was rebuilt a few years later 14 km from its original site, near the village of Cajabamba. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuadorian-war-of-independence">Ecuadorian War of Independence</a> the city first declared independence on 11 November 1820 but was soon retaken by Crown forces. In 1822 it became part of Gran Colombia, and in 1830 the Republic of Ecuador established its own independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Riobamba is the capital of Chimborazo Province, in the Chambo River Valley of the Andes, 200 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> at an elevation of 2,754 m, close to the Chimborazo volcano. The name joins the Spanish rio, &quot;river&quot;, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> rispampa, &quot;plain&quot;. The city keeps much architecture from the Spanish colonial period, and its historic centre has a number of well-preserved churches. Its economy rests on the agriculture of the surrounding region and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranching, and indigenous artisans sell textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leather">leather goods</a> and dairy products at its open-air market.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Riobamba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A constituent congress at <b>Riobamba</b> adopted a first constitution that September and named the new state after the equator, a neutral name that avoided the regional pride attached to Quito.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake of an estimated magnitude 7.6–8.3, the most powerful historical event known in Ecuador, devastated <b>Riobamba</b> and other cities of the Interandean valley, causing between 6,000 and 40,000 casualties.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-4">4 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Archaeology shows a sequence of local pottery traditions, with the Puruhá phase often linked to tombs found near <b>Riobamba</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/puruha">Puruhá</a></li></ul></section>
